issues.description:
## Problem
The CRM creates per-agency keys lazily (D7 a). As designed in v2, two things go wrong:
- the keys would carry the default key policy, so any acc2 admin with kms:* could decrypt;
- an IAM statement on alias/crm/* grants nothing, because KMS does not accept alias ARNs as IAM resources.

Spec F1 requires no access outside the agency, AA included.

## Scope
- Task-role IAM:
  - kms:CreateKey and TagResource only with aws:RequestTag/App = crm and the allowed tag keys;
  - CreateAlias only on alias/crm-dev/*;
  - crypto actions on key/*, conditioned on kms:ResourceAliases alias/crm-dev/* and on the required encryption-context keys;
  - lockout safety check: the CreateKey API requires the supplied key policy to let the CALLING principal make a later PutKeyPolicy unless BypassPolicyLockoutSafetyCheck is true (docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/latest/APIReference/API_CreateKey.html, read 20/10/2026). The task role never gets PutKeyPolicy, so CreateKey with the template and bypass=false is expected to fail. Test both on Dev and record the choice in the ADR: (a) allow CreateKey only with kms:BypassPolicyLockoutSafetyCheck = true, safe because the template's statement (b) keeps the key manageable by the account root and the drift check enforces the template; or (b) a Terraform-managed key-issuer role that creates keys on the CRM's request;
  - never PutKeyPolicy, ScheduleKeyDeletion or DisableKey.
- Key policy template (versioned JSON in the repo, consumed by CRM-11), with three statements:
  - (a) use: the CRM task role, with kms:EncryptionContext:crm_tenant_id;
  - (b) administer: the account root, with no crypto actions;
  - (c) an explicit Deny of Decrypt, ReEncrypt*, GenerateDataKey* and CreateGrant for every principal except the CRM task role and a CRM break-glass role (the pattern of identity_alerts.tf).
- An EventBridge rule -> SNS + an SQS queue that the CRM reads into the agency audit. It fires on:
  - KMS Decrypt by a non-CRM principal;
  - PutKeyPolicy, CreateGrant, ScheduleKeyDeletion or DisableKey on alias/crm-* keys;
  - ecs ExecuteCommand or UpdateService on the CRM service.
- IAM for the nightly drift-check job (GetKeyPolicy on CRM keys).

## Done when
- A Terraform-managed canary key (alias/crm-dev/canary) carries the template. A Decrypt attempt by the admin role is denied by the explicit Deny and raises the alarm (evidence in the issue).
- The IAM policy simulator shows the task role denied CreateKey without aws:RequestTag/App = crm, and the lockout-check choice (a or b above) is recorded with the CreateKey test output. (The 'key created by the CRM role' check is in CRM-11, which owns that code.)
- The Terraform apply through CI is green.

## Problem
Spec module 1: a Stage 1 intake form auto-creates the lead, profile and trip. A first reply under 2 hours needs the agent to be told.

## Scope
- POST /crm/public/v1/intake/{tenant_slug}:
  - Stage 1 fields per spec section 5 (bands, not open numbers);
  - a honeypot;
  - an in-app rate limit keyed on the BFF-signed viewer IP (from CloudFront-Viewer-Address);
  - an idempotency key.
- The crm_intake_process job:
  - dedupes by blind index;
  - creates the traveller (F1), the profile (F2) and a trip at Inquiry;
  - creates the task 'reply < 2 h';
  - emails the agency's agents through the SES transactional configuration set, with a link to the trip and no traveller PII in the body beyond what the agency already owns.
- A migration (next free crm-NNN) for crm.task and crm.metric_event (no PII columns, RLS like crm-001); no earlier issue creates them.
- Metric events intake_submitted and trip_created in crm.metric_event (the baseline table).

## Done when
- Submitting the same payload twice creates one trip, and a burst from one IP is limited.
- On Dev, the synthetic agency's agent mailbox (the e2e mail sub-domain) receives the notification.

## Problem
D12 requires us. inference profiles and zero-retention models, and N10 picked Haiku 5.5. Today:
- the acc3 invoker role trusts only CIS and TripPlanner (accounts/acc3-bedrock/main.tf:21-106);
- it allows only global. profiles (Haiku 4.5, Sonnet 4.6, Sonnet 5, Opus 5.5), with no ApplyGuardrail;
- the Haiku 4.5 list price is 10x that of Haiku 5.5;
- acc2 may block Bedrock through the reseller.

## Scope
- Free read-only checks (no model invocation):
  - get-foundation-model-availability for Haiku 5.5 / Sonnet 5.5 and their us. profiles on acc3 us-west-1;
  - the model invocation logging configuration on acc3 (it must be off for CRM traffic);
  - whether ApplyGuardrail is callable on acc2.
- Write the Terraform for a new role AA3-Bedrock-Invoker-CRM:
  - trust = the CRM task roles, with a new ExternalId;
  - allow only the chosen us. profiles + their foundation-model ARNs in the 3-ARN shape, and ApplyGuardrail on one guardrail ARN.
  The apply waits for the AI wave and needs the acc3 profile with MFA.
- Check whether an application inference profile tagged App=crm is supported for the cost split.

## Done when
- The findings are posted in the issue with the exact CLI outputs (no account ids in public repos).
- If Haiku 5.5 has no us. profile, the issue holds a short decision note for Nghiep: Haiku 4.5 at 10x the price, global., or wait.
- The role Terraform is merged behind a disabled flag, or its PR is open awaiting the AI wave.
